## §6. GOVERNANCE FRAMEWORK

### 6.1 Policy Drift Detection

Failures involving governance semantics MUST be classified as `policy_regression_fix`, not `production_bug_fix`.

**Policy drift triggers:** fail-closed vs fail-open behavior, approval/rejection logic, confidence thresholds, manual review routing, replay enforcement, side-effect guards, mutation gate bypass.

Evidence MUST include `## POLICY_DRIFT` section with: failing test, policy domain, intended vs observed behavior, winning contract, resolution layer, repair plan.

FORBIDDEN: patching assertion to match broken behavior; adjusting threshold to make test pass; classifying governance bug as `production_bug_fix`.

### 6.2 Contract Conflict Escalation

When two rules require incompatible behavior from the same component: STOP, record `## CONTRACT_CONFLICT`, identify winning contract (lower L-number wins, constitutional rules win), name resolution layer. Do NOT resume repair until conflict resolved.

Patching around a contract conflict = HARD FAIL.

### 6.3 Environment Parity

Local environment behavior is not valid CI behavior until an environment contract artifact proves parity.

Required when failure involves: subprocess execution, WSL/OS boundary, CUDA/GPU, HuggingFace/vLLM, import path manipulation, filesystem layout assumptions, network/external services.

Evidence MUST include `## ENVIRONMENT_CONTRACT` section. FORBIDDEN: fixing locally and declaring convergence before CI validation.

### 6.4 C0 Informational Boundary

Informational context (retrieval, memory recall, telemetry, external data) MAY inform decisions but MUST NOT directly mutate routing, policy thresholds, execution authority, or governance controls.

Handoff MUST be explicit (named interface), auditable (signal and effect logged), and governed (control logic applies its own validation).

FORBIDDEN: retrieval output directly setting routing variable; similarity score directly unlocking mutation gate; memory recall directly bypassing manual review.

